          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Page no. 109"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.112,
            0.677,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "**Which of the following could be the other number: 2, 3, 5, 8, 10?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.139,
            0.392,
            0.156
          ],
          "content": "Ans. The other number will be 8."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.166,
            0.234,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "**Page no. 110**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.194,
            0.882,
            0.231
          ],
          "content": "**Q. What jump size can reach both 15 and 30? There are multiple jump sizes possible. Try to find them all.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.241,
            0.533,
            0.286
          ],
          "content": "Ans. Jump size of 3 or 5 will take us to 15 & 30. Other possible jump sizes are 1, 15."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.322,
            0.677,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "**Q.1. Is there anything common among the shaded numbers?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.353,
            0.498,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "Ans. All shaded numbers are multiples of 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.38,
            0.676,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "**Q.2. Is there anything common among the circled numbers?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.41,
            0.496,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "Ans. All circled numbers are multiples of 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.437,
            0.874,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": "**Q.3. Which numbers are both shaded and circled? What are these numbers called?** Ans. 36, 48, 60. These numbers are called common multiples of 3 and 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.522,
            0.218,
            0.539
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Section 5.1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.549,
            0.234,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": "**Page no. 110**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.575,
            0.264,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": "**Figure it out**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.605,
            0.616,
            0.623
          ],
          "content": "**Q.1. Find all multiples of 40 that lie between 310 and 410.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.633,
            0.581,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "Ans. Multiples of 40 that lie between 310 and 410 are: 320, 360, 400."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.687,
            0.263,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": "**Q.2. Who am I?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.714,
            0.881,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": "**a. I am a number less than 40. One of my factors is 7. The sum of my digits is 8. b. I am a number less than 100. Two of my factors are 3 and 5.One of my digits is 1 more than the other.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.788,
            0.226,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": "Ans. a. 35 b. 45"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.927,
            0.506,
            0.94
          ],
          "content": "2"
        }
